# Line endings.
#
# Every text file is stored and checked out with Unix line endings, so a
# worktree on Windows does not show every file as changed. The two rules
# at the end of this file come later and override this one.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Two kinds of file here are read by Windows itself rather than by a
# programmer's editor, and both must have Windows line endings whatever the
# machine they were last edited on.
#
# The batch files are run by the command interpreter, which has a long
# history of parsing oddly when a file arrives with Unix line endings.
#
# The plain text files include the note that travels inside the folder sent
# to other people. Somebody on an older Windows opens it in Notepad and, with
# Unix line endings, gets the whole note as one unbroken line. That is worse
# than untidy for a screen reader, which loses every paragraph break the note
# depends on to be readable.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.txt text eol=crlf
